
# Hadoop Installation Guide

## 1. Introduction

Apache Hadoop is an open-source framework used for distributed storage and processing of large datasets. It provides a distributed file system called Hadoop Distributed File System (HDFS) and a resource management and processing framework through YARN and MapReduce.

This experiment demonstrates the installation and configuration of Apache Hadoop in an Ubuntu environment running through Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL).

## 2. Objective

The objectives of this experiment are:

* To configure a Linux environment using WSL.
* To install Java required for Hadoop.
* To configure SSH for Hadoop.
* To install Apache Hadoop.
* To configure Hadoop environment variables.
* To configure HDFS.
* To configure YARN and MapReduce.
* To initialize the Hadoop NameNode.
* To start and verify Hadoop services.
* To perform basic HDFS operations.

## 3. Environment Used

The Hadoop environment was configured using:

* Host Operating System: Windows
* Linux Environment: Ubuntu on WSL
* Terminal: WSL Ubuntu Terminal
* Hadoop: Apache Hadoop
* Storage: HDFS
* Resource Management: YARN
* Processing Framework: MapReduce
* Java: OpenJDK

The WSL terminal was accessed successfully with the following Linux prompt:

```text
vishnupriya_reddy2-9@LAPTOP-Q0UV996:~$
```

This confirms that the Ubuntu WSL environment was running successfully.

## 4. Update the Ubuntu Package Repository

The Ubuntu package repository was updated before installing the required dependencies.

```bash
sudo apt update
```

The installed packages were also upgraded:

```bash
sudo apt upgrade -y
```

## 5. Install Java

Java is required for running Apache Hadoop.

Java Development Kit was installed using:

```bash
sudo apt install openjdk-8-jdk -y
```

The Java installation was verified using:

```bash
java -version
```

The Java compiler was also verified:

```bash
javac -version
```

## 6. Configure Java Environment

The Java installation directory was identified using:

```bash
readlink -f $(which javac)
```

The Java home directory can be determined using:

```bash
dirname $(dirname $(readlink -f $(which javac)))
```

The Java environment variable was then configured:

```bash
export JAVA_HOME=$(dirname $(dirname $(readlink -f $(which javac))))
```

The configured Java path can be verified using:

```bash
echo $JAVA_HOME
```

## 7. Install SSH

Hadoop uses SSH for communication between Hadoop services and nodes.

OpenSSH was installed using:

```bash
sudo apt install openssh-server openssh-client -y
```

The SSH service was started using:

```bash
sudo service ssh start
```

## 8. Configure SSH for Hadoop

An SSH key pair was generated:

```bash
ssh-keygen -t rsa
```

The public key was added to the authorized keys:

```bash
cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

The required permissions were configured:

```bash
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

The SSH connection to localhost was tested:

```bash
ssh localhost
```

A successful localhost connection confirms that SSH is configured for the Hadoop environment.

## 9. Download and Extract Hadoop

The Apache Hadoop distribution was downloaded and extracted in the WSL Ubuntu environment.

After downloading the Hadoop archive, it was extracted using:

```bash
tar -xzf hadoop-*.tar.gz
```

The Hadoop directory was then configured for use in the environment.

## 10. Configure Hadoop Environment Variables

The Hadoop installation directory was configured using the Hadoop environment variables.

The following variables were configured:

```bash
export HADOOP_HOME=$HOME/hadoop
export HADOOP_HDFS_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export HADOOP_MAPRED_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export YARN_HOME=$HADOOP_HOME
export PATH=$PATH:$HADOOP_HOME/bin
export PATH=$PATH:$HADOOP_HOME/sbin
```

The environment variables were loaded using:

```bash
source ~/.bashrc
```

The Hadoop installation was verified using:

```bash
hadoop version
```

## 11. Hadoop Configuration Directory

The Hadoop configuration files are located inside:

```text
$HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/
```

The main configuration files used are:

```text
hadoop-env.sh
core-site.xml
hdfs-site.xml
mapred-site.xml
yarn-site.xml
```

## 12. Configure hadoop-env.sh

The Hadoop environment configuration file was opened using:

```bash
nano $HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/hadoop-env.sh
```

The Java home was configured in the file:

```bash
export JAVA_HOME=$(dirname $(dirname $(readlink -f $(which javac))))
```

This allows Hadoop to locate the Java installation.

## 13. Configure core-site.xml

The core Hadoop configuration file was opened using:

```bash
nano $HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/core-site.xml
```

The following configuration was used:

```xml
<configuration>

    <property>
        <name>fs.defaultFS</name>
        <value>hdfs://localhost:9000</value>
    </property>

</configuration>
```

The `fs.defaultFS` property defines the default HDFS NameNode address.

## 14. Configure hdfs-site.xml

Directories for the Hadoop NameNode and DataNode were created:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/hadoop_data/namenode
mkdir -p ~/hadoop_data/datanode
```

The HDFS configuration file was opened:

```bash
nano $HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/hdfs-site.xml
```

The configuration was defined as:

```xml
<configuration>

    <property>
        <name>dfs.replication</name>
        <value>1</value>
    </property>

    <property>
        <name>dfs.namenode.name.dir</name>
        <value>file:///home/vishnupriya_reddy2-9/hadoop_data/namenode</value>
    </property>

    <property>
        <name>dfs.datanode.data.dir</name>
        <value>file:///home/vishnupriya_reddy2-9/hadoop_data/datanode</value>
    </property>

</configuration>
```

The replication factor was set to `1` because this experiment uses a single-node Hadoop environment.

## 15. Configure MapReduce

The MapReduce configuration file was created from the template:

```bash
cp $HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/mapred-site.xml.template $HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/mapred-site.xml
```

The configuration file was opened:

```bash
nano $HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/mapred-site.xml
```

The following configuration was used:

```xml
<configuration>

    <property>
        <name>mapreduce.framework.name</name>
        <value>yarn</value>
    </property>

</configuration>
```

This configures MapReduce to run using YARN.

## 16. Configure YARN

The YARN configuration file was opened:

```bash
nano $HADOOP_HOME/etc/hadoop/yarn-site.xml
```

The following configuration was used:

```xml
<configuration>

    <property>
        <name>yarn.nodemanager.aux-services</name>
        <value>mapreduce_shuffle</value>
    </property>

    <property>
        <name>yarn.nodemanager.aux-services.mapreduce_shuffle.class</name>
        <value>org.apache.hadoop.mapred.ShuffleHandler</value>
    </property>

</configuration>
```

This enables the MapReduce shuffle service required for MapReduce execution.

## 17. Format the NameNode

Before using HDFS for the first time, the NameNode was formatted:

```bash
hdfs namenode -format
```

The formatting process initializes the HDFS metadata required by the NameNode.

NameNode formatting should only be performed when initializing a new HDFS environment because formatting an existing NameNode can remove its existing filesystem metadata.

## 18. Start HDFS

The Hadoop Distributed File System services were started using:

```bash
start-dfs.sh
```

The running Hadoop processes were verified using:

```bash
jps
```

The expected HDFS services include:

```text
NameNode
DataNode
SecondaryNameNode
```

## 19. Start YARN

YARN services were started using:

```bash
start-yarn.sh
```

The services were verified using:

```bash
jps
```

The expected YARN services include:

```text
ResourceManager
NodeManager
```

## 20. Verify HDFS

The HDFS root directory was checked using:

```bash
hdfs dfs -ls /
```

A test directory was created:

```bash
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /test
```

The directory was verified:

```bash
hdfs dfs -ls /
```

Successful execution confirms that HDFS is operational.

## 21. Test Hadoop MapReduce

A sample MapReduce program provided with Hadoop can be executed to verify the MapReduce environment.

```bash
hadoop jar $HADOOP_HOME/share/hadoop/mapreduce/hadoop-mapreduce-examples-*.jar pi 10 1000
```

Successful execution confirms that the Hadoop MapReduce framework is functioning correctly.

## 22. Verify Hadoop Services

The Hadoop Java processes were checked using:

```bash
jps
```

A successfully running single-node Hadoop environment should show services such as:

```text
NameNode
DataNode
SecondaryNameNode
ResourceManager
NodeManager
```

## 23. Hadoop Web Interfaces

The Hadoop NameNode web interface can be accessed through:

```text
http://localhost:9870/
```

The YARN ResourceManager interface can be accessed through:

```text
http://localhost:8088/
```

These interfaces provide information about HDFS storage, DataNodes, applications, and cluster resources.

## 24. Stop Hadoop Services

YARN services can be stopped using:

```bash
stop-yarn.sh
```

HDFS services can be stopped using:

```bash
stop-dfs.sh
```

The services can be verified using:

```bash
jps
```

## 25. Verification

The following checks were performed during the Hadoop installation:

1. Ubuntu WSL environment was successfully accessed.
2. Java was installed and verified.
3. SSH was installed and configured.
4. SSH localhost connectivity was tested.
5. Hadoop was installed.
6. Hadoop environment variables were configured.
7. Hadoop configuration files were configured.
8. NameNode was formatted.
9. HDFS services were started.
10. YARN services were started.
11. Hadoop processes were verified using `jps`.
12. HDFS commands were executed successfully.
13. MapReduce functionality was tested.

## 26. Result

Apache Hadoop was successfully installed and configured in an Ubuntu environment running through Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL).

The Hadoop single-node environment was configured with HDFS, YARN, and MapReduce. The Hadoop services and basic HDFS operations were verified successfully.

The configured environment can be used for subsequent Big Data Analytics experiments involving HDFS, MapReduce, Apache Spark, Cassandra, MongoDB, Neo4j, and other Big Data technologies.
